WEBVTT

00:00.000 --> 00:09.800
On the course website, you will find a handy list of test case assertions here at the bottom,

00:09.800 --> 00:17.800
so equals not equals a certain true asserting false. Using the standard library unit tests,

00:17.800 --> 00:23.440
it's fine for most projects, even have a reasonable size, but as your programs go,

00:23.440 --> 00:28.880
as your code organization becomes more complex, you might want to consider a more advanced

00:28.880 --> 00:34.480
alternate testing framework or test runner. There's a third-party tool called Knows

00:34.480 --> 00:40.160
and a third-party testing framework called Pytest that are very popular. Those modules are

00:40.160 --> 00:46.080
compatible with unit tests, but they do things slightly differently. And once you feel like

00:46.080 --> 00:51.920
your unit tests are getting out of control, it's a good way to, that's a good stopping point

00:51.920 --> 00:54.240
to do a little bit of experimentation.

